Notebook[{Cell["Lepton-quark scattering with transverse spin transfer, step \
3: scalar integrals", "Title"], Cell["Input: Results/<route>/Algebra.wl from \
step 2 (choose the route below).  Output: Results/<route>/Reduced.wl with the \
real-emission phase-space integrals and the virtual loop integral evaluated \
as Laurent series in eps, still exact in the threshold variable.  The spin \
average and the transverse spin transfer are treated together; step 4 turns \
them into the finite hard coefficients.  This file is that of \
Processes/LeptonQuark (read its explanations there): the same integral \
families, reductions and master integrals apply, only the integrands \
differ.\n\nMethod.  The unobserved two-particle phase space of lp and r is \
written as cut propagators (reverse unitarity), so that integration-by-parts \
reduction with Kira applies.  With pRec = l + k - p = lp + r, pRec^2 = s4, \
the cut propagators are r^2 and (pRec - r)^2 = lp^2.  The uncut denominators \
of the real matrix element are a = (k - r)^2, b = (p + r)^2 and tau = (k - p \
- r)^2 = a + b + t.  Since tau depends on a and b, only two of the three can \
appear in one integral; partial fractions give three families:\n\n   \
real_ab[1,1,n3,n4]    = integral of 1/(a^n3 b^n4)     over the cut,\n   \
real_a_tau[1,1,n3,n4] = integral of 1/(a^n3 tau^n4),\n   \
real_b_tau[1,1,n3,n4] = integral of 1/(b^n3 tau^n4),\n\ndefined in \
kira/real/config/integralfamilies.yaml (indices 1 and 2 are the two cut \
propagators and always equal 1).  The virtual family is \
VirtualIntegral[n0,n1,n2] with denominators loop^2, (loop-k)^2, (loop-p)^2, \
in kira/virtual.  Kira reduces every integral to four phase-space masters \
(the two-body volume and the angular integral in three kinematic \
configurations) and one loop master (the off-shell bubble).  Their values \
follow from one-dimensional Euler integrals evaluated with \
SubTropica.\n\nDimension shifts.  The polarized matrix element contains the \
hatted invariant spe3 = rhat.rhat.  A power spe3^n under the phase-space \
integral equals, up to the factor (4 Pi)^n (-1)^n (-eps)_n, the same integral \
in D + 2n dimensions ((x)_n is the Pochhammer symbol).  Kira's reduction \
holds for any dimension d, so the shifted integrals are obtained by setting d \
-> 4 + 2n - 2 eps in its rules; the masters in D + 2 are evaluated \
separately.", "Text"], Cell["Load", "Section"], 
  Cell["Get[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"..\", \"..\", \"FeynFacet\", \
\"FeynFacet.wl\"}]]", "Input"], Cell["route = \"FeynCalc\";        (* \
\"FeynCalc\", \"FORM\" or \"Tracer\": which step-2 result to reduce \
*)\nalgebra = LoadResult[\"Algebra\", route];\nspins = {\"Unpolarized\", \
\"Transverse\"};\nKeys[algebra]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Real emission: partial fractions into the three families", 
   "Section"], Cell["Remove the common factor CF ee^4 eq^2 gs^2, write D = 4 \
+ dm4 and u = s4 - s - t, and decompose the squared matrix element into terms \
with at most two of the denominators a, b, tau = a + b + t.  Different from \
the lepton-quark process, the transverse tensor has numerator powers of the \
invariants beyond what the denominators absorb (the normal-component \
reduction multiplies by rpar^2, quadratic in a and b), so the terms are \
allowed to carry numerators: a term x^(-i) y^(-j) with x, y the two \
denominators of a family and i or j negative is the family integral with a \
negative index (Kira handles them; kira/real/jobs.yaml allows numerator \
degree s = 4).  The denominators are first written through inert symbols fA, \
fB, fT; a term with all three is split with 1/(a b tau) = [1/(a b) - 1/(a \
tau) - 1/(b tau)]/t; then, for each term, the numerator (a polynomial in a \
and b) is rewritten in the two denominators of its family (b = tau - a - t in \
real_a_tau, a = tau - b - t in real_b_tau).", "Text"], 
  Cell["forms = <|fA -> a, fB -> b, fT -> a + b + t|>;\nformOf[factor_] := \
Module[{matches = Select[Keys[forms],\n    Expand[factor - forms[#]] === 0 || \
Expand[factor + forms[#]] === 0 &]},\n  Require[Length[matches] == 1, \"A \
denominator factor is not a, b or tau\", factor];\n  First[matches] \
If[Expand[factor - forms[First[matches]]] === 0, 1, -1]];\ntoForms[expr_] := \
Module[{mapped = MapIntegrationDenominators[expr,\n    {tau -> a + b + t}, \
{a, b}, formOf]},\n  Require[!FailureQ[mapped], \"Termwise denominator \
conversion failed\", mapped];\n  mapped /. {a -> fA, b -> fB}];\ntripleRule = \
fA^i_. fB^j_. fT^k_. /; i < 0 && j < 0 && k < 0 :>\n  fA^(i + 1) fB^(j + 1) \
fT^(k + 1) (1/(fA fB) - 1/(fA fT) - 1/(fB fT))/t;\ntermList[expr_] := With[{e \
= Expand[expr]}, Which[e === 0, {}, Head[e] === Plus, List @@ e, True, \
{e}]];\nnegativeForms[term_] := Select[Keys[forms], Exponent[term, #] < 0 \
&];\n(* Only the unit propagator geometry is split; its scalar coefficient is \
a spectator. *)\npartialFractionMonomial[unit_] := Module[{split},\n  split = \
FixedPoint[Expand[# /. tripleRule] &, unit, 20];\n  \
Require[AllTrue[termList[split], Length[negativeForms[#]] <= 2 &], \"Partial \
fractions did not terminate\", unit];\n  Require[Together[(split - unit) /. \
forms] === 0, \"A geometric partial fraction does not reconstruct\", unit];\n \
 split];\nfractionConverter = CreatePartialFractionConverter[Keys[forms], \
spe3, partialFractionMonomial];\nrealStripped = Association @ Table[spin ->\n \
 Expand[algebra[\"Real\"][spin]/(CF ee^4 eq^2 gs^2) /. {D -> 4 + dm4, u -> s4 \
- s - t}], {spin, spins}];\nrealFractionResults = Association @ Table[spin -> \
fractionConverter[toForms[realStripped[spin]]], {spin, \
spins}];\nRequire[FreeQ[realFractionResults, _Failure], \"Sparse partial \
fractions failed\", realFractionResults];\nrealFractionTerms = Association @ \
Table[spin -> realFractionResults[spin][\"Terms\"], {spin, spins}];\nLength \
/@ realFractionTerms", "Input"], Cell["Check: the terms of every spin case \
add up to the matrix element when the integrals are read as their integrands \
(each row was already checked against its term; this checks the triple \
splitting).", "Text"], Cell["familyForms = <|KRealAB -> {fA, fB}, KRealATau \
-> {fA, fT}, KRealBTau -> {fB, fT}|>;\nfamilyOf[denominators_List] := \
Which[SubsetQ[{fA, fB}, denominators], KRealAB,\n  SubsetQ[{fA, fT}, \
denominators], KRealATau, SubsetQ[{fB, fT}, denominators], KRealBTau,\n  \
True, Require[False, \"A term has three denominators\", \
denominators]];\nfamilySolutions = Association @ KeyValueMap[Function[{head, \
pair},\n  head -> First[Solve[{X == forms[pair[[1]]], Y == forms[pair[[2]]]}, \
{a, b}]]], familyForms];\nintegrand[row_] := row[\"Coefficient\"] \
dm4^row[\"Dm4Power\"] spe3^row[\"HatPower\"] Times @@\n  MapThread[#1^(-#2) \
&, {forms /@ familyForms[Head[row[\"Integral\"]]], List @@ \
row[\"Integral\"][[3 ;; 4]]}];\nreconstructRows[term_, rows_List] := \
Together[Total[integrand /@ rows] - (term /. forms)] === 0;\nunitRows[unit_] \
:= Module[{den, head, pair, powers, numerator, poly, rows},\n  den = \
negativeForms[unit]; head = familyOf[den]; pair = familyForms[head];\n  \
powers = Association @ Table[f -> -Exponent[unit, f], {f, den}];\n  numerator \
= unit Times @@ Table[f^(-Exponent[unit, f]), {f, den}] /. forms;\n  poly = \
Expand[numerator /. familySolutions[head]];\n  Require[PolynomialQ[poly, {X, \
Y, dm4, spe3}], \"A numerator is not polynomial in its family\", unit];\n  \
rows = Table[<|\"Integral\" -> head[1, 1, Lookup[powers, pair[[1]], 0] - \
rule[[1, 1]],\n      Lookup[powers, pair[[2]], 0] - rule[[1, 2]]], \
\"Dm4Power\" -> rule[[1, 3]],\n    \"HatPower\" -> rule[[1, 4]], \
\"Coefficient\" -> rule[[2]]|>,\n    {rule, CoefficientRules[poly, {X, Y, \
dm4, spe3}]}];\n  Require[reconstructRows[unit, rows], \"A unit integral \
mapping does not reconstruct\", unit]; rows];\nrowVariables = \
Join[Keys[forms], {dm4, spe3}];\nrowConverter = \
CreateIntegralRowConverter[rowVariables, unitRows, reconstructRows];\n(* \
Separate evanescent numerator powers before mapping, retaining trace D = 4 + \
dm4. *)\nrowInputs = Association @ Table[spin -> \
CombineLaurentTerms[Total[realFractionTerms[spin]], rowVariables], {spin, \
spins}];\nRequire[FreeQ[rowInputs, _Failure], \"Laurent coefficient grouping \
failed\", rowInputs];\nrealRowResults = Association @ Table[spin -> \
rowConverter[rowInputs[spin]], {spin, spins}];\nRequire[FreeQ[realRowResults, \
_Failure], \"Integral-row conversion failed\", realRowResults];\nrealTerms = \
Association @ Table[spin -> realRowResults[spin][\"Rows\"], {spin, \
spins}];\nindexRanges = <|\"denominators\" -> Max[Prepend[Total[Select[List \
@@ #[\"Integral\"], # > 0 &]] & /@ Flatten[Values[realTerms]], 0]],\n  \
\"numerators\" -> Max[Prepend[-Total[Select[List @@ #[\"Integral\"], # < 0 \
&]] & /@ Flatten[Values[realTerms]], \
0]]|>;\nRequire[indexRanges[\"denominators\"] <= 6 && \
indexRanges[\"numerators\"] <= 4,\n  \"The integrals exceed the shipped Kira \
index ranges\", indexRanges];\n(* Together verifies this identity; it is not \
used to build the integration rows. *)\nDo[Require[Together[Total[integrand \
/@ realTerms[spin]] - (realStripped[spin] /. tau -> a + b + t)] === 0,\n  \
\"The family terms do not add up to the matrix element\", spin], {spin, \
spins}];\nDataset[realTerms[\"Transverse\"]]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Reduction with Kira", "Section"], Cell["The YAML files define the \
families; the target list is written from the integrals that actually occur.  \
Kira sets s = 1 in the real families and t = 1 in the virtual family \
(symbol_to_replace_by_one); RestoreScale puts the scale back.  Kira's family \
names real_ab, real_a_tau, real_b_tau, virtual are spelled KRealAB, \
KRealATau, KRealBTau, KVirtual in Mathematica.", "Text"], 
  Cell["kiraNames = {KRealAB -> \"real_ab\", KRealATau -> \"real_a_tau\", \
KRealBTau -> \"real_b_tau\", KVirtual -> \"virtual\"};\nvirtualFamily = \
algebra[\"VirtualFamily\"] /. VirtualIntegral -> \
KVirtual;\nreferenceRealMasters = {KRealAB[1, 1, 0, 0], KRealAB[1, 1, 1, 1], \
KRealATau[1, 1, 1, 1], KRealBTau[1, 1, 1, 1]};\nreferenceVirtualMasters = \
{KVirtual[0, 1, 1]};\nrealTargets = Union[Flatten[Lookup[realTerms[#], \
\"Integral\"] & /@ spins], referenceRealMasters];\nvirtualTargets = \
Union[Cases[virtualFamily, _KVirtual, Infinity], \
referenceVirtualMasters];\nkiraReal = \
PrepareKira[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"kira\", \"real\"}], \
FileNameJoin[{ResultsDirectory[route], \"kira\", \"real\"}]];\nkiraVirtual = \
PrepareKira[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"kira\", \"virtual\"}], \
FileNameJoin[{ResultsDirectory[route], \"kira\", \
\"virtual\"}]];\nWriteKiraTargets[kiraReal, realTargets, \
kiraNames];\nWriteKiraTargets[kiraVirtual, virtualTargets, \
kiraNames];\n{realTargets, virtualTargets}", "Input"], 
  Cell["RunKira[kiraReal];\nRunKira[kiraVirtual];\nrealRules = \
RestoreScale[Join @@ Table[KiraRules[kiraReal, name, kiraNames],\n  {name, \
Select[{\"real_ab\", \"real_a_tau\", \"real_b_tau\"},\n    \
FileExistsQ[FileNameJoin[{kiraReal, \"results\", #, \"kira_targets.m\"}]] \
&]}],\n  s, {t -> t/s, s4 -> s4/s}, {KRealAB, KRealATau, \
KRealBTau}];\nvirtualRules = RestoreScale[KiraRules[kiraVirtual, \"virtual\", \
kiraNames], t, {}, {KVirtual}];\nCheckReductionCoverage[realTargets, \
realRules, {KRealAB, KRealATau, KRealBTau}, \
referenceRealMasters];\nCheckReductionCoverage[virtualTargets, virtualRules, \
{KVirtual}, referenceVirtualMasters];\nColumn[Join[virtualRules, realRules]]"\
, "Input"], Cell["Every real integral reduces to real_ab[1,1,0,0] (the \
phase-space volume) and the three angular integrals real_ab[1,1,1,1], \
real_a_tau[1,1,1,1], real_b_tau[1,1,1,1]; the virtual family reduces to the \
off-shell bubble virtual[0,1,1].  The reduction rules are in the dimension d; \
a term with spe3^n uses them at d = 4 + 2n - 2 eps.  KiraMaster[family, \
indices, shift] denotes a master in D + shift dimensions.", "Text"], 
  Cell["masterHeads = {KRealAB -> \"real_ab\", KRealATau -> \"real_a_tau\", \
KRealBTau -> \"real_b_tau\"};\nrealReduced = Association @ Table[spin -> \
Collect[Expand[Total[Map[Function[row, With[{n = row[\"HatPower\"]},\n  \
row[\"Coefficient\"] (-2 eps)^row[\"Dm4Power\"] (4 Pi)^n (-1)^n \
Pochhammer[-eps, n] *\n    ((row[\"Integral\"] /. realRules /. d -> 4 + 2 n - \
2 eps) /.\n      (head : KRealAB | KRealATau | KRealBTau)[indices__] :> \
KiraMaster[head /. masterHeads, {indices}, 2 n])]],\n  realTerms[spin]]]], \
_KiraMaster, Together], {spin, spins}];\n(* Trace coefficients retain their \
original D; only the reduction rules use d. *)\nvirtualReduced = \
Factor[Together[virtualFamily /. D -> 4 - 2 eps /. virtualRules /. d -> 4 - 2 \
eps]];\nRequire[FreeQ[realReduced, KRealAB | KRealATau | KRealBTau] && \
Union[Cases[virtualReduced, _KVirtual, Infinity]] === {KVirtual[0, 1, 1]},\n  \
\"Some integrals were not reduced\"];\n{Union[Cases[realReduced, _KiraMaster, \
Infinity]], virtualReduced}", "Input"], Cell["Master integrals", "Section"], 
  Cell["The phase-space masters are the two-body volume Phi2 and the angular \
integral\n\n   Omega(z) = 2 (1-2eps) integral_0^infinity dx x^(-1-eps) \
(1+x)^eps/(z+x),\n\nwhere z is the cross ratio of the two uncut invariants of \
each family (zAB, zAT, zBT below).  At the threshold w = 1 the cross ratio \
zAB vanishes, and Omega(z) behaves like z^(-1-eps): this is where the plus \
distributions come from, so this behaviour must be kept exact instead of \
being expanded in eps.  Omega is a Gauss hypergeometric function, and the \
standard connection formula between the arguments 1-z and z separates the two \
behaviours at z = 0:\n\n   Omega(z) = -2 (1-2eps)/eps [ Gamma(1-eps) \
Gamma(1+eps) (1-z)^eps z^(-1-eps) + Gamma(1-eps) Gamma(-1-eps)/Gamma(-eps)^2 \
F1(z) ],   F1(z) = 2F1(1,1;2+eps;z),\n\nand in D + 2 dimensions, Omega2(z) = \
2 (3-2eps) integral dx x^(-eps) (1+x)^(eps-1)/(z+x),\n\n   Omega2(z) = 2 \
(3-2eps)/(1-eps) [ Gamma(2-eps) Gamma(eps) (1-z)^(eps-1) z^(-eps) + \
Gamma(2-eps) Gamma(-eps)/Gamma(1-eps)^2 F2(z) ],   F2(z) = \
2F1(1,1;1+eps;z).\n\nThe first terms are exact; F1 and F2 are regular at z = \
0 and are evaluated as Euler integrals, F1(z) = (1+eps) integral dy \
(1+y)^(-1-eps)/(1+(1-z)y) and F2(z) = eps integral dy (1+y)^(-eps)/(1+(1-z)y) \
over (0, infinity) (the substitution t = y/(1+y) in Euler's integral \
representation of 2F1).  Phi2 and the loop bubble are Euler integrals as \
well.\n\nEulerIntegral[prefactor, integrand, variables, parameters, order, \
eps] integrates with SubTropica and returns the expansion in eps through the \
given order.  The volume Phi2 is needed two orders deeper than the angular \
functions because the latter come with a 1/eps collinear pole and the \
threshold expansion adds a second pole.", "Text"], 
  Cell["LoadSubTropica[]", "Input"], Cell["masters = <|\n  \"bubble\" -> \
EulerIntegral[Gamma[eps], x^(-eps) (1 + x)^(2 eps - 2), {x}, {}, 1, eps],\n  \
\"phaseSpace\" -> EulerIntegral[1/Gamma[1 - eps], x^(-eps) (1 + x)^(2 eps - \
2), {x}, {}, 2, eps],\n  \"phaseSpaceD2\" -> EulerIntegral[1/Gamma[2 - eps], \
x^(1 - eps) (1 + x)^(2 eps - 4), {x}, {}, 1, eps],\n  \"F1\" -> \
EulerIntegral[1 + eps, (1 + y)^(-1 - eps)/(1 + zb y), {y}, {zb}, 2, eps] /. \
zb -> 1 - z,\n  \"F2\" -> EulerIntegral[eps, (1 + y)^(-eps)/(1 + zb y), {y}, \
{zb}, 2, eps] /. zb -> 1 - z|>", "Input"], Cell["Checks.  The two-body \
volumes are Beta functions, Gamma(1-eps)/Gamma(2-2eps) and \
Gamma(2-eps)/Gamma(4-2eps); the massless bubble is Gamma(eps) \
Gamma(1-eps)^2/Gamma(2-2eps).  F1 and F2 are compared with Mathematica's \
Hypergeometric2F1: since the latter cannot be expanded in eps symbolically, \
we verify that the difference between the exact function and the truncated \
series scales like eps^3 (the difference at eps/2 must be one eighth of that \
at eps), which tests every computed order.  Finally the assembled Omega(z) \
and Omega2(z) are compared with direct numerical integrations in the same way \
(the truncation error is of order eps^3 for Omega and eps^2 for Omega2, whose \
regular branch carries a 1/eps prefactor).", "Text"], 
  Cell["check[x_] := Require[Simplify[x] === 0, \"Master integral check \
failed\", x];\ncheck[Normal[Series[masters[\"phaseSpace\"] - Gamma[1 - \
eps]/Gamma[2 - 2 eps], {eps, 0, \
2}]]];\ncheck[Normal[Series[masters[\"phaseSpaceD2\"] - Gamma[2 - \
eps]/Gamma[4 - 2 eps], {eps, 0, \
1}]]];\ncheck[Normal[Series[masters[\"bubble\"] - Gamma[eps] Gamma[1 - \
eps]^2/Gamma[2 - 2 eps], {eps, 0, 1}]]];\ntruncationCheck[series_, exact_, \
power_] := Module[{d1, d2},\n  d1 = N[(exact - series) /. {eps -> -1/50, z -> \
1/3}, 30];\n  d2 = N[(exact - series) /. {eps -> -1/100, z -> 1/3}, 30];\n  \
Require[Abs[d2/d1 - 2^-power] < 2^-power/10, \"Master integral disagrees with \
its closed form\", {d1, d2}]; {d1, d2}];\ntruncationCheck[masters[\"F1\"], \
Hypergeometric2F1[1, 1, 2 + eps, z], 3];\ntruncationCheck[masters[\"F2\"], \
Hypergeometric2F1[1, 1, 1 + eps, z], 3]", "Input"], 
  Cell["(* F1 and F2 are kept as inert symbols F1[n][z] and F2[n][z], the \
coefficients of\n   eps^n, until the end of step 4: the expressions stay \
small, and at the\n   threshold their values are exact, F1[0][0] = F2[0][0] = \
1 and F1[n][0] =\n   F2[n][0] = 0 for n > 0, because 2F1(1,1;c;0) = 1 for \
every c. *)\nangular[zz_] := -2 (1 - 2 eps)/eps (Gamma[1 - eps] Gamma[1 + \
eps] (1 - zz)^eps zz^(-1 - eps) +\n  Gamma[1 - eps] Gamma[-1 - \
eps]/Gamma[-eps]^2 Sum[eps^n F1[n][zz], {n, 0, 2}]);\nangularD2[zz_] := 2 (3 \
- 2 eps)/(1 - eps) (Gamma[2 - eps] Gamma[eps] (1 - zz)^(eps - 1) zz^(-eps) \
+\n  Gamma[2 - eps] Gamma[-eps]/Gamma[1 - eps]^2 Sum[eps^n F2[n][zz], {n, 0, \
2}]);\nmasterFunctions = Join[{F1[0][0] -> 1, F2[0][0] -> 1, F1[_][0] -> 0, \
F2[_][0] -> 0},\n  Table[F1[n][zz_] :> \
Evaluate[SeriesCoefficient[masters[\"F1\"], {eps, 0, n}] /. z -> zz], {n, 0, \
2}],\n  Table[F2[n][zz_] :> Evaluate[SeriesCoefficient[masters[\"F2\"], {eps, \
0, n}] /. z -> zz], {n, 0, 2}]];\nnumericCheck[expr_, integrand_, power_] := \
Module[{d1, d2},\n  d1 = N[expr /. {eps -> -1/40, z -> 1/3}, 20] - \
NIntegrate[integrand /. {eps -> -1/40, z -> 1/3}, {x, 0, Infinity}, \
WorkingPrecision -> 30];\n  d2 = N[expr /. {eps -> -1/80, z -> 1/3}, 20] - \
NIntegrate[integrand /. {eps -> -1/80, z -> 1/3}, {x, 0, Infinity}, \
WorkingPrecision -> 30];\n  Require[Abs[d2/d1 - 2^-power] < 2^-power/10, \
\"Angular master disagrees with direct integration\", {d1, d2}]; {d1, \
d2}];\nnumericCheck[angular[z] /. masterFunctions, 2 (1 - 2 eps) x^(-1 - eps) \
(1 + x)^eps/(z + x), 3];\nnumericCheck[angularD2[z] /. masterFunctions, 2 (3 \
- 2 eps) x^(-eps) (1 + x)^(eps - 1)/(z + x), 2]", "Input"], 
  Cell["Insert the physical masters", "Section"], 
  Cell["Normalization of the phase-space integrals: with measure d^D r/(2 \
Pi)^(D-1) delta+(r^2) 2 Pi delta+((pRec - r)^2), the volume is\n\n   Phi2 = \
(4 Pi)^eps s4^(-eps)/(8 Pi) Gamma(1-eps)/Gamma(2-2eps),\n\nand in D + 2 \
dimensions Phi2' = (4 Pi)^(eps-1) s4^(1-eps)/(8 Pi) \
Gamma(2-eps)/Gamma(4-2eps).  The angular masters are Phi2 times Omega(z) \
divided by the product of the two invariants at the symmetric point, with the \
cross ratios\n\n   zAB = -t s4/((s+t)(s-s4)),   zAT = -t \
(s+t-s4)/((s+t)(s4-t)),   zBT = -s t/((s-s4)(s4-t)),\n\nall between 0 and 1 \
in the physical region.  The loop master is the bubble with measure d^D \
loop/(i Pi^(D/2)): (-t)^(-eps) Gamma(eps) Gamma(1-eps)^2/Gamma(2-2eps).", 
   "Text"], Cell["phaseSpace = (4 Pi)^eps s4^(-eps)/(8 Pi) \
masters[\"phaseSpace\"];\nphaseSpaceD2 = (4 Pi)^(eps - 1) s4^(1 - eps)/(8 Pi) \
masters[\"phaseSpaceD2\"];\nzAB = -t s4/((s + t) (s - s4));  zAT = -t (s + t \
- s4)/((s + t) (s4 - t));  zBT = -s t/((s - s4) (s4 - t));\nphysicalMasters = \
{\n  KiraMaster[\"real_ab\", {1, 1, 0, 0}, 0] -> phaseSpace,\n  \
KiraMaster[\"real_ab\", {1, 1, 1, 1}, 0] -> -phaseSpace angular[zAB]/((s + t) \
(s - s4)),\n  KiraMaster[\"real_a_tau\", {1, 1, 1, 1}, 0] -> phaseSpace \
angular[zAT]/((s + t) (s4 - t)),\n  KiraMaster[\"real_b_tau\", {1, 1, 1, 1}, \
0] -> -phaseSpace angular[zBT]/((s - s4) (s4 - t)),\n  \
KiraMaster[\"real_ab\", {1, 1, 0, 0}, 2] -> phaseSpaceD2,\n  \
KiraMaster[\"real_ab\", {1, 1, 1, 1}, 2] -> -phaseSpaceD2 angularD2[zAB]/((s \
+ t) (s - s4)),\n  KiraMaster[\"real_a_tau\", {1, 1, 1, 1}, 2] -> \
phaseSpaceD2 angularD2[zAT]/((s + t) (s4 - t)),\n  KiraMaster[\"real_b_tau\", \
{1, 1, 1, 1}, 2] -> -phaseSpaceD2 angularD2[zBT]/((s - s4) (s4 - \
t))};\nrealIntegrated = Association @ Table[spin -> CF ee^4 eq^2 gs^2 \
(realReduced[spin] /. physicalMasters), {spin, spins}];\nvirtualIntegrated = \
virtualReduced /. KVirtual[0, 1, 1] -> (-t)^(-eps) \
masters[\"bubble\"];\nRequire[FreeQ[{realIntegrated, virtualIntegrated}, \
KiraMaster | KVirtual], \"A master integral was not \
inserted\"];\nSeries[virtualIntegrated, {eps, 0, 0}]", "Input"], 
  Cell["The virtual form factor (times the Born square) has the familiar \
poles -2/eps^2 - 3/eps.  The real-emission result is kept with its exact \
threshold factors s4^(-eps), zAB^(-1-eps) and zAB^(-eps) and with the inert \
symbols F1[n][z], F2[n][z]; the replacement rules masterFunctions, saved with \
